# Rust
/target/
**/*.rs.bk

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/

# maturin / PyO3 development artefacts
# `dockpy.dll` is the raw cargo cdylib; it is NOT the importable extension.
# `maturin develop` or a wheel build produces the real `_dockpy` module.
dockpy.dll
*_dockpy.pyd
*_dockpy.so
*_dockpy.dylib

# Build output
/dist/
/dist-gpu/

# `scripts/workbench_interaction_check.py` used to write its negative-coordinate
# receptor here, on every run. It is a derived fixture -- `rec_prep.pdbqt` with
# every x shifted by -40, regenerable byte-identically -- so it is a generated
# artefact and was never an input this repository ships. It is written under
# `target/`, which the anchored target entry above already covers, so it needs no
# line of its own; a literal here would name a path that can no longer exist and
# would read, to anyone auditing it, as a shipped input. (The token is spelled
# without its leading slash on purpose: `published_set_floor_check.py` mutates that
# one anchored occurrence to an unanchored one, and a second copy in this comment
# would make the mutation ambiguous rather than make it wrong.)

# Output of the CLI, not source. `examples/maps/` alone is 40 .map files and
# ~45 MB; all three are reproduced by running `rec-grid` / `dock` / `split`.
# `examples/ligands/` IS source: the verification scripts read from it.
examples/maps/
examples/multi/
examples/split_dir/

# PowerShell writes a file literally named `$null` when a `rm` array is not
# expanded, and it has turned up three times in two sessions -- once staged,
# once deleted, once back. It is never source and never wanted.
$null
